{
  "teamId": 112,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Chicago Cubs arrived with genuine expectations, and April validated every bit of the offseason optimism. Powered by a retooled lineup featuring Alex Bregman and Michael Conforto alongside homegrown talents, the Cubs tore through April at 17-9, announcing themselves as legitimate contenders. Big moments came early and often — Bregman's clutch single on April 11th, Busch's two-run shot on the 12th, Conforto's timely double, and Moisés Ballesteros's grand slam capping a stretch of early-season dominance that felt like a statement to the rest of the NL.\n\nMay and June told a more complicated story. The Cubs stumbled to 13-16 in May, a skid fueled in part by bullpen implosions — Phil Maton and Ryan Rolison giving up back-to-back home runs in a single humbling afternoon. Yet June brought the club roaring back at 16-10, largely on the breakout of Pete Crow-Armstrong, whose bat turned electric. His home run on June 6th — his 11th of the year — was the exclamation point on what was becoming a star-making season. Jordan Wicks induced a key double play in a tight late-June game, and the Cubs looked like a team that had found its identity.\n\nJuly and August were the heart of the Cubs' season, a grinding stretch where they posted winning records (14-10, 16-12) despite a bullpen that kept threatening to unravel everything. Jacob Webb became the season's recurring nightmare, surrendering walk-off blasts and crushing late-game leads with alarming regularity. The Trent Thornton implosion on July 29th erased a Pete Crow-Armstrong home run in the same game. And yet the offense kept answering — Bregman, Happ, Hoerner, and PCA kept manufacturing enough runs to stay afloat, and the Cubs staggered into the postseason at 89-73 with something to prove.\n\nThe Wild Card round against the Padres proved to be a two-game nightmare. Matthew Boyd was punished by Fernando Tatis Jr. and Manny Machado in Game 1, surrendering home runs and extra-base hits that swung the contest decisively. Game 2 offered a flicker of hope — Nico Hoerner's single plated Seiya Suzuki to keep the Cubs breathing — but Daniel Palencia's late-inning Gavin Sheets homer extinguished any comeback and ended Chicago's season. A 17-9 April had promised so much; a two-game playoff exit delivered the familiar sting of an 89-win team that ran out of runway at exactly the wrong moment.",
